## Rows skipped (with reason)
  a#3  004 link label «artikkel 10 i næringsavgiftsloven (營業稅法)» → «næringsavgiftsloven (營業稅法)».
       The Korean source itself labels this flno=3 link «영업세법 제10조» and the English column
       «Article 10 of the Value-Added and Non-Value-Added Business Tax Act». The mismatch between
       the label and flno=3 is in the source, not in nb. Dropping the 10 also removes a number the
       `numbers` check counts. Left as is; this is a source-level item for the attorney/supervisor.
  a#10 (main part) «I praksis idømmes i dag som regel omkring 3 måneders fengsel».
       RULEBOOK-FIX R3 prescribes exactly the wording that is in the file («i enkelte dommer …;
       det finnes ikke noe typisk eller garantert utfall»), and ACCEPTED-ROUND forbids making a
       result sentence positive again. Only the reviewer's second point was applied (the 4- and
       6-month sentence had dropped «fengsel»).
  a#19 003 (and the same in 005, 006, 008, 009): delete the U+200B blank lines / promote Q16–Q20 to «## » headings.
  b#16 013/015: delete every U+200B.
       `checkBlocks` compares `countZwsp(source) !== countZwsp(target)` — the Korean source carries
       the identical 52/8/4/10/5/5/3 zero-width spaces, so deleting them FAILs `blocks`. Promoting
       Q16+ to headings also fails `headings` (the Korean and English sources keep Q16–Q20 as plain
       paragraphs). Both are checker/source-pinned, not an nb defect.
  a#31 001–004, 006, 007 and b#8 010, 011, 017, 018: period thousands → space thousands.
       ACCEPTED-ROUND skips space separators, and the checker confirms why:
       `NUMBER_THOUSAND_STYLE.nb = 'dot'`, and a space-grouped «500 000» is read by the `\d+` pass as
       500 and 000, so every grouped amount FAILs `numbers`. Dot grouping kept everywhere in the columns.
       (The guidance pack's «1 570 000 TWD» is outside this checker and was left untouched.)
  b#34 018: renumber «## 7.» to «## 6.» and «## 8.» to «## 7.».
       The reviewer notes the English source has the same skip; so does the Korean source
       (`## 5.`, `## 7.`, `## 8.`). ACCEPTED-ROUND forbids changing numbers, and the renumbering
       would diverge from every other locale. Source-level item.

